Identifying causal effects within hierarchical linear models using sampled data presents a significant challenge. This issue is particularly pronounced in models incorporating latent variables, a scenario frequently encountered in epidemiological research, where data is often collected in case-control formats. This study addresses the specific complexities arising from a binary outcome variable and either a binary or polytomous latent covariate. Focusing on a mediation framework that includes an interaction term, we first define the conditions under which all parameters can be nonparametrically identified using case-control data. Subsequently, we present a maximum likelihood estimation function tailored for this mediation model with interaction using such sampled data. Finally, we evaluate the function's performance through simulations and apply it to an epidemiological dataset. Our analytical findings indicate that the binary nature of the case-control outcome does not hinder the nonparametric identification of the mediation model with interaction when the mediator is binary. However, we observe that while point estimation is achievable without bias, standard errors and associated inference (p-values) for the latent binary mediator and the interaction term between the exposure and mediator remain biased. We recommend that researchers remain cautious when utilizing p-values for statistical inference regarding these specific parameters when analyzing case-control data. Furthermore, we have made our code available for users to apply this methodology to their own data. Code for users with a binary latent variable:
\url{https://github.com/JulianSchell/mediationmodelCaseControlData}
Code for users with a polytomous latent variable:
\url{https://github.com/JulianSchell/mediationmodelCaseControlDataPoly}
\textit{Note: While the source code is public, please be aware that the GitHub repository was created prior to the formal derivation of the theoretical results presented in this manuscript.} Additionally, please refer to our blog post for an accessible explanation of our results:
\url{https://jschell.github.io/mediationmodelCaseControlData/}
